body:
Deciding what to use means noticing that the three inputs converge more than any of them flags. All three independently arrive at the same process: approach the withdrawing flatmate one-to-one, keep the ask small, avoid one grand meeting meant to settle everything. The account reaches this at its step 4, the objection reaches it by promoting the conversational-form reading, the rival reaches it by demanding separate conversations. A recommendation that survives three incompatible diagnoses is worth more than any of the diagnoses, so I take the process as settled and treat everything above it as contested.

The real dispute is what the first move presupposes, and here I side with the objection's epistemics without adopting its reordering. The account opened with a specification, which presupposes the ambiguity reading; the objection would promote conversational form to the front; the rival would open with a question about what kind of household this is. But the objection's own point — thin material licenses probes, not sequences — cuts symmetrically: no reordering is better supported than the original ordering. The way through is not a better sequence but a first probe cheap enough to be worth running under every reading. The rival supplied one almost incidentally, in its second test: when a proposal is floated, watch whether the withdrawing flatmate engages with its content or pulls back from its very form. That observation presupposes nothing, and paired with the account's recurrence-tracking it yields the discriminating test the objection asked for and the account's original test could not be:

- engages with content, recurrence on the specified item falls — the ambiguity reading earns its keep;
- engages, but the same dispute recurs despite a clear spec — it was never about what the words meant; renegotiation or legitimacy gains;
- engages, recurrence falls, but new complaint items appear — displacement, the objection's own prediction, pointing at load or relationship;
- pulls back from the form of any proposal — the rival's reading gains; the issue is being bound, not being unclear;
- no engagement even one-to-one — the channel itself, or something outside the chores, is the problem.

What I keep from the account, with a defense it did not make: the fallback rule for schedule changes is not only an ambiguity remedy. It is exactly what the objection's renegotiation example needs — 'you said you'd do the bins' answered by 'my shift changed' is a dispute a pre-agreed fallback rule settles in advance. That tool survives even if the objection's alternative is the right one. What no specification answers is legitimacy — who has standing to raise a breach — and there a written arrangement can make things worse by formalizing enforcement. Which is where the rival's remedy turns out stronger than the rival's diagnosis. Owned zones and default rotations can be explicit about outcomes while removing standing to audit, addressing the legitimacy and exposure worries at once. The lesson I would draw: the coercive element in these arrangements is not writtenness, it is auditability. Honest limit: zones fit divisible items well and genuinely shared surfaces badly; for a shared bathroom, rotation-plus-fallback remains the least-bad shape.

What I downgrade: the rival's opening move. Asking 'what do you take this flat to be' hands the participant already leaving the conversation an abstract, high-stakes question. I keep the aim and change the timing and form: ask it concretely and late, if the cheap probes warrant — and treat the unit-versus-privacy dichotomy as a probe, not a taxonomy, since households routinely mix mutual obligation on hygiene-critical items with privacy everywhere else. In the other direction, the objection's own concession stands: if the flatmates genuinely cannot state what they agreed to, ambiguity is the plainest reading. It is demoted from default to unconfirmed, not rejected.

Two things no input put on the table. First, whatever the withdrawing flatmate says one-to-one must not reappear in the group as 'well, she told me...' — that channel dies the first time it is quoted, which is why the rival's keep-the-conversations-separate commitment is the piece I adopt most firmly. Second, the problem says friends, and all three contributions optimize the household's functioning without asking what you are optimizing. The living-arrangement question takes different right answers depending on whether your priority is a working flat or intact friendships; moving out can preserve a friendship that a year of chore litigation would end. Decide that before the bigger conversation, not during it.

Concretely, then: (1) alone, reconstruct one recent dispute and ask which of three things it was about — what the words meant, whether the deal still bound after the schedule change, or who had standing to raise it; (2) one-to-one with the avoiding friend, an open question first — what have these arguments actually been about from your side — with no proposal in the first instance; (3) if structure is welcome, one item with a fallback rule, framed as provisional and unaudited, then watch the two channels for a couple of weeks; (4) if form-pullback or non-engagement persists, name the living-arrangement question explicitly — the account's step 5 and the rival's final move agree there — after deciding what you want it to answer.

The caveat the objection would be right to press on me too: this remains construction on four sentences. The matrix is worth running because it is cheap and generates information, not because it is validated; if the avoidance concerns something outside the household entirely, every cell misreads.
